  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 1224 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nThe main upper level trough over the western US is forecasted to\nshear out and lift north, with a shortwave moving across the\nPanhandle into Oklahoma and Texas late Saturday. This pushes a\ncold front through the area Saturday Night, with CAMs again\nshowing a band of showers and maybe a thunderstorm developing\nalong the front Saturday Night into early Sunday morning. Right\nnow, CAMs are focusing on low POPs (20-30%) across the Hill County\nand Heartland. \n\nCooler and drier behind the front with afternoon highs dropping\nback into the 50s for much of next week. Another shortwave will\nmove into the Plains and then close off as it drops from Oklahoma\ninto Texas. This will likely be east of the area and that would\nplace West Central Texas on the dry side of the low. It will allow\na reinforcing surge of cooler air for Tuesday and this will drop \na surface cold front into the area for mid week. Right now, model \nblends are showing lows in the mid and upper 30s, but these models\noften don't handle colder radiational cooling nights well. \nWhichever night ends up with the center of the surface high closer\nto the area, and the lighter winds that go with it, will likely \nsee a decent radiational cooling set up and thus colder \ntemperatures than what is currently forecasted. Best guess would \nbe for Wednesday morning, but thats far enough out that the day \nmay change. \n\nModels diverge fairly significantly for next weekend, with the GFS\nshowing a large closed off upper low near El Paso and a large area\nof rainfall across West and Central Texas, with the ECMWF much\nweaker and progressive and a largely dry forecast. At this point,\nwill let the model blends split the difference and show some \nsmall POPs across the area.",
